# R39 fmt collection-literal interior comment escape — KNOWN GAP:
# a comment INTERIOR to the generic-args slot of a struct/function
# instantiation (`Foo[T, # C, U](a)`) is NOT covered by the round's fix.
#
# The R39 `Expr::StructLiteral` arm's dispatch narrows the interior-check
# to the ARG-tuple range (from `args.first().span.start` to `expr.span.end`)
# specifically to AVOID mis-firing on a generic_args-interior comment
# with the wrong container_end (arg-tuple end, not generic-args end). But
# this correctness fix also leaves the generic-args-interior escape
# UNCAUGHT. Similar for `Expr::Call` and `format_generic_args_wrapped`.
#
# INTENDED (post-fix) shape after `gg fmt`:
#     void main():
#         auto x = foo[
#             int,
#             # in generic args
#             String,
#         ](42)
#         print(x)
#
# CURRENT shape:
#     void main():
#         auto x = foo[int, String](42)
#         # in generic args   ← escapes to enclosing scope
#         print(x)
#
# UN-IGNORE + PROMOTE to a live regression fixture when
# `format_generic_args_wrapped` grows the same interior-comment dispatch
# (small addition — reuse `has_interior_comments` and
# `format_bracketed_broken_with_comments` with `[`/`]`). Additionally,
# `format_generic_params_wrapped` (for struct/fn generic parameters
# `struct Foo[T, U]:`) has the same shape and should be updated together.

Vector[int] foo[T, U](int x):
    return [x]

void main():
    auto x = foo[
        int,
        # in generic args
        String,
    ](42)
    print(x[0])
